{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789838456,"nonce":"7ll+zd/mSSOzfoNY1fQFVqx+gIg=","message":"Barrier spike landed in 922dda3, behind ASH_GC_PROTECT=1 (unix only), collector unchanged. The design from the previous comment: after a collection every block the sweep kept whole (minus the blocks threads are bumping through and blocks with recyclable free lines) is mprotect'ed read-only; a chained SIGSEGV/SIGBUS handler, installed lazily after every other handler so it runs first, marks the block dirty and restores PROT_WRITE; the next collection lifts everything and counts.\n\nNumbers, NUC, one binary, interleaved with a wrapper that sets the env var (ash_bench.py --ash-base, 7 reps, hybrid-auto and full-jit, whole default corpus, 44/44 OK): protection on is within 0..4% of off on every row, and a control run with a NO-OP wrapper shows the same band (deltablue -4.6%, map_simple -8.4% between two identical binaries), so the tax is below what the harness can resolve. binary_trees: 403.5 vs 407.4ms hybrid-auto, 407.1 vs 405.1 full-jit. Pause total on binary_trees 38.3 -\u003e 42.9ms over 6 collections (the ~400 mprotect calls inside the stop; runs are coalesced but the kept blocks are not contiguous).\n\nWhat the dirty bits say, which is the point: binary_trees protects 384..467 blocks per cycle and dirtied SEVEN blocks across the whole run. The live set is old AND clean; sticky marks would re-trace next to nothing of it. Conformance unit/sys/threads on interp/hybrid/jit with the switch on: 1372/1372.\n\nThe hazard, confirmed from the code rather than a failure: a syscall that writes straight into a protected block gets EFAULT, not a fault. File reads are safe by accident (file.rs reads through a 64KB Rust-side buffer, then copies; strace shows read(2) never targets the heap). hlp_socket_recv calls recv() straight into the GC buffer and process.rs read_pipe likewise, so a long-lived receive buffer that survives a collection would break the next recv. Bytes buffers are MEM_KIND_NOPTR and the allocator throws that kind away at hl_gc_alloc_gen (nothing else records it), so the fix that makes this shippable is kind-segregated blocks: pointer-free allocations in their own blocks, never protected -- which also stops the conservative marker scanning every byte buffer word by word. That segregation is the next step before the sticky-mark half, and worth measuring on its own for marking time.","files":null}]}